      "locator": {"page": 178, "equation": "8.48", "figure": null, "panel": null, "section": "8.9 Decompositions of cyclic structures"},
      "confidence": 0.5,
      "curator_note": "Upper branch off the ring-opened distonic ion, labelled 'alpha', giving the small heteroalkene radical cation CH2=Y+. (formaldehyde radical cation, m/z 30, for Y=O); the complementary neutral (C3H6) is NOT drawn on the page. Book draws 0 electron arcs for this branch (only the 'alpha'-labelled reaction arrow and the product ion CH2=Y+.). This is charge retention on the heteroatom fragment and, unlike the ethylene-loss branch (8.50), it is NOT a single radical-site alpha-cleavage of the drawn distonic ion (the radical and the C-O bond that must break are 1,3-related); semantics is therefore net_fragmentation. The 3 fishhooks encoded here are curator-supplied for electron balance and depict a plausible single-step route (intramolecular homolytic substitution: the tail radical closes onto the carbon bearing the O, expelling CH2=O+. and forming the C-C bond of the neutral). Cyclopropane is the curator-chosen closed-shell C3H6 neutral (1,3-ring closure of the two radical termini, no H-transfer needed); propene would require an undrawn H-shift."
    },
